4
00:01:04,464 --> 00:01:07,810
Your witness, Mr. Moran.

5
00:01:07,834 --> 00:01:10,480
Thank you, Your Honor.

6
00:01:13,607 --> 00:01:20,990
♪ Miss Snyder, you are guilty
of murder in the first degree ♪

7
00:01:21,014 --> 00:01:23,126
♪ We have six
witnesses who swear ♪

8
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:27,230
♪ They saw you at
the murder scene ♪

9
00:01:27,254 --> 00:01:34,604
♪ For your face is
so very appealing ♪

10
00:01:34,628 --> 00:01:40,743
♪ This crime shows a
heart without feeling ♪

11
00:01:40,767 --> 00:01:43,212
♪ I object! ♪

12
00:01:43,236 --> 00:01:44,814
♪ Your Honor ♪

13
00:01:44,838 --> 00:01:48,384
♪ This badger is out of order ♪

14
00:01:48,408 --> 00:01:50,453
♪ Overruled! ♪

15
00:01:50,477 --> 00:01:51,688
♪ He must be right ♪

16
00:01:51,712 --> 00:01:52,789
♪ He must be right ♪

17
00:01:52,813 --> 00:01:53,923
♪ He must be right ♪

18
00:01:53,947 --> 00:01:54,924
♪ He must be right ♪

19
00:01:54,948 --> 00:01:55,859
♪ He must be right ♪

20
00:01:55,883 --> 00:02:00,029
♪ She must be the guilty one ♪

21
00:02:00,053 --> 00:02:02,887
All right, I'm guilty.

22
00:02:04,091 --> 00:02:05,616
But he deserved it.

23
00:02:07,928 --> 00:02:09,305
Order. Order!

24
00:02:09,329 --> 00:02:11,107
He did it.

25
00:02:11,131 --> 00:02:15,545
♪ I'm overruled ♪

26
00:02:15,569 --> 00:02:16,980
♪ I'm a fool ♪

27
00:02:17,004 --> 00:02:18,615
♪ For justice is blind ♪

28
00:02:18,639 --> 00:02:20,049
♪ Oh, no ♪

29
00:02:20,073 --> 00:02:22,485
♪ And so is love ♪

30
00:02:22,509 --> 00:02:23,920
♪ Your love ♪

31
00:02:23,944 --> 00:02:25,455
♪ A wonderful time ♪

32
00:02:25,479 --> 00:02:27,090
♪ You're mine ♪

33
00:02:27,114 --> 00:02:34,230
♪ I want a life sentence,
no chance for repeal ♪

34
00:02:34,254 --> 00:02:37,233
♪ For love and justice ♪

35
00:02:37,257 --> 00:02:40,503
♪ Love and justice ♪

36
00:02:40,527 --> 00:02:41,904
♪ Justice... ♪

37
00:02:41,928 --> 00:02:44,240
♪ Justice ♪

38
00:02:44,264 --> 00:02:52,264
♪ Love is blind! ♪

39
00:03:10,223 --> 00:03:11,501
What'd you think?

40
00:03:11,525 --> 00:03:13,903
Well, it's, uh...

41
00:03:13,927 --> 00:03:16,639
It-It... It's got potential.

42
00:03:16,663 --> 00:03:18,655
You can see that.

43
00:03:22,869 --> 00:03:24,881
You didn't like it.

44
00:03:24,905 --> 00:03:26,449
May I be honest with you?

45
00:03:26,473 --> 00:03:27,617
Of course.

46
00:03:27,641 --> 00:03:29,585
How could you let
them use your name

47
00:03:29,609 --> 00:03:30,887
as technical advisor?

48
00:03:30,911 --> 00:03:32,255
That bad?

49
00:03:32,279 --> 00:03:33,589
Oh, awful!

50
00:03:33,613 --> 00:03:35,958
Julie, I've known this
writer since he was six.

51
00:03:35,982 --> 00:03:38,828
We've got to think of
something nice to say to him.

52
00:03:38,852 --> 00:03:41,364
Now, what can we say to him?

53
00:03:43,757 --> 00:03:44,934
Ben.

54
00:03:44,958 --> 00:03:46,335
Oh, hi.

55
00:03:46,359 --> 00:03:49,906
Hi. Julie, this is Sam Spelvin.

56
00:03:49,930 --> 00:03:51,240
- Hi, Sam.
- Pleasure to meet you.

57
00:03:51,264 --> 00:03:53,943
- Congratulations.
- Thank you. Thank you.

58
00:03:53,967 --> 00:03:55,311
I can't believe

59
00:03:55,335 --> 00:03:58,848
one person could have
written all those lyrics

60
00:03:58,872 --> 00:04:00,149
and all that dialogue.

61
00:04:00,173 --> 00:04:01,417
Yeah.

62
00:04:01,441 --> 00:04:02,952
And composed all that music.

63
00:04:02,976 --> 00:04:04,387
Well, it wasn't easy.

64
00:04:04,411 --> 00:04:06,389
Yeah? Yeah.

65
00:04:06,413 --> 00:04:08,124
Sit down, Julie.

66
00:04:08,148 --> 00:04:09,492
Yup.

67
00:04:09,516 --> 00:04:11,194
So, what did you think, Ben?

68
00:04:11,218 --> 00:04:13,881
Be honest.

69
00:04:14,921 --> 00:04:16,899
Well, uh...

70
00:04:16,923 --> 00:04:20,036
it-it's... it's got, uh...
it's got potential.

71
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:22,655
Now you can see that.

72
00:04:24,431 --> 00:04:27,492
Isn't that the star?

73
00:04:29,536 --> 00:04:32,506
That's Sally Ayn White.

74
00:04:33,774 --> 00:04:35,752
Oh, my God.

75
00:04:35,776 --> 00:04:37,053
What's the matter?

76
00:04:37,077 --> 00:04:38,221
Over there.

77
00:04:38,245 --> 00:04:40,056
That man.

78
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:41,524
It's John Bosley Hackett.

79
00:04:41,548 --> 00:04:43,526
Was he in the show?

80
00:04:43,550 --> 00:04:45,962
He's that critic on TV.

81
00:04:45,986 --> 00:04:48,765
The most powerful
critic in the country.

82
00:04:48,789 --> 00:04:51,634
His reviews can kill
a show in one night.

83
00:04:51,658 --> 00:04:52,935
Oh.

84
00:04:52,959 --> 00:04:54,670
He must have seen
the performance tonight.

85
00:04:54,694 --> 00:04:57,440
Oh, my God, I-I'm having
a nervous breakdown. I...

86
00:04:57,464 --> 00:04:59,642
Well, wasn't it
just a rehearsal?

87
00:04:59,666 --> 00:05:01,965
No, it... it was a preview.

88
00:05:03,003 --> 00:05:06,430
You're not the only
one who's upset.

89
00:05:09,075 --> 00:05:10,787
Guy looks apoplectic.

90
00:05:10,811 --> 00:05:11,954
Who's Guy?

91
00:05:11,978 --> 00:05:13,122
Producer.

92
00:05:13,146 --> 00:05:14,357
Oh.

93
00:05:14,381 --> 00:05:16,058
He hated it. I know it.

94
00:05:16,082 --> 00:05:17,860
Uh, I'll be right back.

95
00:05:17,884 --> 00:05:19,113
Excuse me.

96
00:05:25,659 --> 00:05:27,503
Mr. Hackett, I'm Sam Spelvin.

97
00:05:27,527 --> 00:05:28,738
I, uh, work...

98
00:05:28,762 --> 00:05:30,807
Mr. Hackett knows who you are,

99
00:05:30,831 --> 00:05:33,109
and you don't want
to shake his hand.

100
00:05:33,133 --> 00:05:36,379
He's going to kill us
on his show tomorrow.

101
00:05:36,403 --> 00:05:39,849
You killed yourselves
with this dreadful disaster

102
00:05:39,873 --> 00:05:42,752
you call a musical comedy.

103
00:05:42,776 --> 00:05:44,253
You can't review the play yet.

104
00:05:44,277 --> 00:05:45,655
I'm still working on it.

105
00:05:45,679 --> 00:05:47,657
You're charging for tickets.

106
00:05:47,681 --> 00:05:50,810
I am reviewing your... play.

107
00:05:59,526 --> 00:06:01,604
Yeah... uh-huh...

108
00:06:06,266 --> 00:06:07,743
It's a good one. Yeah.

109
00:06:07,767 --> 00:06:09,045
I hate this dress.

110
00:06:09,069 --> 00:06:11,113
I look like a sunflower.

111
00:06:11,137 --> 00:06:14,183
But you are sunshine and
hope in a ruthless world.

112
00:06:14,207 --> 00:06:15,685
Can't I be death?

113
00:06:15,709 --> 00:06:17,019
I look great in black.

114
00:06:17,043 --> 00:06:18,454
You look great in everything.

115
00:06:18,478 --> 00:06:19,622
Angela.

116
00:06:19,646 --> 00:06:21,290
Get a shot of her in this.

117
00:06:21,314 --> 00:06:23,526
Mm.

118
00:06:23,550 --> 00:06:24,694
Great. Great.

119
00:06:24,718 --> 00:06:25,862
Sound guy, come on.

120
00:06:25,886 --> 00:06:27,463
I want to have one
for my scrapbook.

121
00:06:27,487 --> 00:06:28,965
Come on, everybody.
It's getting late.

122
00:06:28,989 --> 00:06:31,000
After Hackett's review, we
may never see each other again.

123
00:06:31,024 --> 00:06:32,335
Uh-huh.

124
00:06:32,359 --> 00:06:33,669
Maybe Hacket'll change his mind.

125
00:06:33,693 --> 00:06:35,171
Dream on, pal.

126
00:06:35,195 --> 00:06:36,572
You know, this is so wrong.

127
00:06:36,596 --> 00:06:39,008
I'm gonna go this morning,
I'm gonna talk to him

128
00:06:39,032 --> 00:06:40,877
and try to convince
him to hold off.

129
00:06:40,901 --> 00:06:42,945
Why don't you concentrate
on fixing the show

130
00:06:42,969 --> 00:06:44,714
instead of John
Bosley Hackett's review.

131
00:06:44,738 --> 00:06:45,882
Okay, thank you.

132
00:06:45,906 --> 00:06:47,550
Enough fun.
Enough... A lot of fun.

133
00:06:47,574 --> 00:06:49,619
Let's go here. We
got a show to put on.

134
00:06:49,643 --> 00:06:51,120
Let's, uh, get through
the bloody murder.

135
00:06:51,144 --> 00:06:53,055
Okay, let's go. Bring in
the murder prop. Come on.

136
00:06:53,079 --> 00:06:54,290
I'll be in

137
00:06:54,314 --> 00:06:56,125
- my dressing room, Tommy.
- Yeah, I'll...

138
00:06:56,149 --> 00:06:57,793
I'll call you when I need you.

139
00:06:57,817 --> 00:06:58,995
All right, everybody. Love you.

140
00:06:59,019 --> 00:07:01,364
Everybody up, up, up.

141
00:07:01,388 --> 00:07:03,833
Energy. I'll be
upstairs watching.

142
00:07:08,228 --> 00:07:10,373
Places, ladies and gentlemen.

143
00:07:10,397 --> 00:07:13,576
Okay, Brian, we can do
this whenever you're ready.

144
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:16,846
Three. Go.

145
00:08:50,130 --> 00:08:51,807
Nice.

146
00:08:51,831 --> 00:08:53,376
Ni-Nice work, everybody.

147
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:54,543
Thank you.

148
00:08:54,567 --> 00:08:55,745
Take a break.

149
00:08:55,769 --> 00:08:57,279
Sam, uh, you stay right there.

150
00:08:57,303 --> 00:08:59,415
I'm gonna come down,
we'll talk about the cuts.

151
00:08:59,439 --> 00:09:01,117
I like it the way it is.

152
00:09:01,141 --> 00:09:02,385
The show is overlong,

153
00:09:02,409 --> 00:09:05,855
and that number is so
long, it stops the show dead.

154
00:09:05,879 --> 00:09:07,323
You're out of your mind.

155
00:09:07,347 --> 00:09:10,292
The way you've staged
it is what stops it dead.

156
00:09:10,316 --> 00:09:12,128
Listen to you.

157
00:09:12,152 --> 00:09:14,663
Maybe you ought to tell
that to John Bosley Hackett

158
00:09:14,687 --> 00:09:15,931
when you see him.

159
00:09:15,955 --> 00:09:17,600
Yeah, well, maybe I will.

160
00:09:17,624 --> 00:09:19,668
Fine. Brian?

161
00:09:19,692 --> 00:09:21,070
Yeah.

162
00:09:21,094 --> 00:09:22,838
Would you get me some
coffee, please? And call

163
00:09:22,862 --> 00:09:25,508
Sally Ayn, and we'll run the
"Innocence" number from the top.

164
00:09:25,532 --> 00:09:27,160
- Thanks.
- All right.

165
00:09:56,996 --> 00:09:58,974
He's dead.

166
00:10:03,002 --> 00:10:04,980
<i>I know I was found</i>

167
00:10:05,004 --> 00:10:08,884
over the body, and the
murder weapon was mine.

168
00:10:08,908 --> 00:10:13,322
And, uh, I certainly
had a motive.

169
00:10:13,346 --> 00:10:15,758
But I swear,
Ben, I didn't do it.

170
00:10:15,782 --> 00:10:17,960
Somebody's trying to frame me.

171
00:10:17,984 --> 00:10:20,629
Hmm. Okay.

172
00:10:20,653 --> 00:10:23,199
Okay, let's assume that's true.

173
00:10:23,223 --> 00:10:26,435
Uh, who knew you were
going up to see Mr. Hackett?

174
00:10:26,459 --> 00:10:28,037
Everybody.

175
00:10:28,061 --> 00:10:31,507
Sally Ayn White, uh, Guy
Palmer, Tommy DeLuca.

176
00:10:31,531 --> 00:10:34,710
And they all had, uh, a
lot to lose if the show failed.

177
00:10:34,734 --> 00:10:35,945
Yes.

178
00:10:35,969 --> 00:10:37,246
And they all hated Hackett.

179
00:10:37,270 --> 00:10:39,949
And any one of them could
have taken my letter opener.

180
00:10:39,973 --> 00:10:43,119
But weren't they
all in the theater

181
00:10:43,143 --> 00:10:44,854
when you went up to see him?

182
00:10:44,878 --> 00:10:46,989
Yes.

183
00:10:47,013 --> 00:10:48,845
Could any one of them
have left rehearsal?

184
00:10:50,683 --> 00:10:52,661
Of course.

185
00:10:52,685 --> 00:10:55,197
Oh.

186
00:10:55,221 --> 00:11:01,403
Ben, about your fee...
Um, I can't exactly

187
00:11:01,427 --> 00:11:03,239
afford it out of
pocket right now,

188
00:11:03,263 --> 00:11:05,174
so I was hoping we'd, uh,

189
00:11:05,198 --> 00:11:07,276
be able to make
some sort of deal.

190
00:11:07,300 --> 00:11:10,546
A deal?

191
00:11:10,570 --> 00:11:13,149
Yeah.

192
00:11:13,173 --> 00:11:14,416
You put up your services...

193
00:11:14,440 --> 00:11:16,418
A hundred thousand
dollars worth of services.

194
00:11:16,442 --> 00:11:19,488
Right. And

195
00:11:19,512 --> 00:11:21,991
for you investment,
you get $200,000 back

196
00:11:22,015 --> 00:11:24,894
if the show is a hit.

197
00:11:24,918 --> 00:11:26,962
Well, what do I... what do I get

198
00:11:26,986 --> 00:11:29,732
if-if... if the show's
what you call a...

199
00:11:29,756 --> 00:11:32,134
a, mm, flop?

200
00:11:32,158 --> 00:11:34,423
Nothing.

201
00:11:39,599 --> 00:11:41,310
Oh, well, I've...

202
00:11:41,334 --> 00:11:45,581
I've known you since you was,
uh... since you were six, huh?

203
00:11:45,605 --> 00:11:47,583
Yeah. My, uh...

204
00:11:47,607 --> 00:11:50,719
My daddy used to say that
you would hold me on your lap,

205
00:11:50,743 --> 00:11:53,389
and you would tell me stories
that would make me laugh,

206
00:11:53,413 --> 00:11:57,111
and that's where I got
my yen for show business.

207
00:11:59,252 --> 00:12:02,698
Oh, I guess I'm stuck.

208
00:12:02,722 --> 00:12:06,535
Well, I'll, I'll try to get
you out of here, and...

209
00:12:06,559 --> 00:12:10,606
you go to work on that... play.

210
00:12:10,630 --> 00:12:13,676
Now, that first act lays
there like a beached whale.

211
00:12:13,700 --> 00:12:16,579
Ben, I, uh... I
really appreciate...

212
00:12:16,603 --> 00:12:20,301
And the second act lays
there like two beached whales.

213
00:12:31,818 --> 00:12:35,164
Though John Bosley
Hackett died yesterday,

214
00:12:35,188 --> 00:12:37,399
his words and his intelligence
will be with us forever.

215
00:12:37,423 --> 00:12:38,767
Now, in a special tribute,

216
00:12:38,791 --> 00:12:41,437
we present
Mr. Hackett's final review

217
00:12:41,461 --> 00:12:44,840
recorded yesterday just
hours before his untimely death.

218
00:12:44,864 --> 00:12:48,510
Hello. Well, here it is.

219
00:12:48,534 --> 00:12:51,013
The play we've
all been waiting for.

220
00:12:51,037 --> 00:12:53,382
<i>Lovers and Lawyers</i> at
the Brookhaven Theater.

221
00:12:53,406 --> 00:12:55,184
Well, doesn't sound bad.

222
00:12:55,208 --> 00:12:58,520
<i>Lovers and Lawyers</i> makes
this reviewer's life simple.

223
00:12:58,544 --> 00:13:02,625
In the future, when I discuss
the theater's best plays,

224
00:13:02,649 --> 00:13:05,261
I shall continue to vacillate.

225
00:13:05,285 --> 00:13:08,764
But when I'm asked
about the theater's worst,

226
00:13:08,788 --> 00:13:11,600
I shall reply,
without hesitation,

227
00:13:11,624 --> 00:13:14,236
<i>Lovers and Lawyers.</i>

228
00:13:14,260 --> 00:13:15,738
It doesn't sound good.

229
00:13:15,762 --> 00:13:17,940
<i>Lovers and Lawyers</i>
is being directed

230
00:13:17,964 --> 00:13:22,177
haphazardly by Tommy DeLuca,

231
00:13:22,201 --> 00:13:25,114
a has-been who never
was and whose idea

232
00:13:25,138 --> 00:13:30,719
of direction is to allow the
actors to mill around aimlessly.

233
00:13:30,743 --> 00:13:34,156
Rather like chickens
looking for corn.

234
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:38,394
The production is so
tacky and lacking in taste

235
00:13:38,418 --> 00:13:42,898
that it can only be produced
by that master of cheap,

236
00:13:42,922 --> 00:13:45,267
Guy Palmer.

237
00:13:45,291 --> 00:13:48,737
Written by Sam Spelvin,
the script would have to be

238
00:13:48,761 --> 00:13:53,709
greatly improved to
be called amateurish.

239
00:13:53,733 --> 00:13:57,813
The play stars aging
ingenue, Sally Ayn White,

240
00:13:57,837 --> 00:14:00,716
in a turgid performance.

241
00:14:04,977 --> 00:14:08,857
I know you're all
disappointed, but...

242
00:14:08,881 --> 00:14:10,359
What's going on?

243
00:14:10,383 --> 00:14:12,995
Guy's closing the show.

244
00:14:13,019 --> 00:14:16,031
Oh, oh, Sam, I'm... I'm sorry.

245
00:14:16,055 --> 00:14:19,001
I know, I know how
much you wanted...

246
00:14:19,025 --> 00:14:22,338
This means I'm not
gonna get paid, doesn't it?

247
00:14:22,362 --> 00:14:23,872
And I wish I could say that

248
00:14:23,896 --> 00:14:25,474
the show must go on.

249
00:14:25,498 --> 00:14:26,809
Hold everything!

250
00:14:26,833 --> 00:14:28,243
Wait a minute!

251
00:14:29,435 --> 00:14:30,646
Here, read this!

252
00:14:30,670 --> 00:14:33,282
Here you go, here you go.

253
00:14:33,306 --> 00:14:35,284
Here you go. Here, read this.

254
00:14:35,308 --> 00:14:37,186
Guy, read this.

255
00:14:37,210 --> 00:14:38,754
It's a miracle.

256
00:14:38,778 --> 00:14:41,190
We snagged victory
from the jaws of defeat.

257
00:14:41,214 --> 00:14:44,426
Um, we have the biggest
advance sales since <i>Miss Saigon.</i>

258
00:14:44,450 --> 00:14:46,228
The publicity
campaign I kicked off

259
00:14:46,252 --> 00:14:48,163
is gonna take us
right up over the top.

260
00:14:48,187 --> 00:14:49,565
What does it say?

261
00:14:49,589 --> 00:14:52,201
Playwright kills
over bad review.

262
00:14:52,225 --> 00:14:54,136
I, um, I wrote the
headline myself.

263
00:14:54,160 --> 00:14:57,106
"Sam Spelvin,
author and composer

264
00:14:57,130 --> 00:14:59,007
"of the bound for
Broadway musical,

265
00:14:59,031 --> 00:15:01,543
<i>"Lovers and Lawyers,</i>
was arrested yesterday

266
00:15:01,567 --> 00:15:04,380
"on suspicion of
murdering national TV critic,

267
00:15:04,404 --> 00:15:05,948
John Bosley Hackett."

268
00:15:05,972 --> 00:15:08,917
Talk about not being
able to take a little criticism.

269
00:15:08,941 --> 00:15:10,219
Sally.

270
00:15:10,243 --> 00:15:12,755
They're lined up for
tickets around the block.

271
00:15:12,779 --> 00:15:15,791
Oh, uh, in case you didn't know,

272
00:15:15,815 --> 00:15:17,059
this is our press agent,

273
00:15:17,083 --> 00:15:19,294
- Sidney Falco.
- Hi.

274
00:15:19,318 --> 00:15:21,630
Let's hear it for Sid.

275
00:15:21,654 --> 00:15:23,232
Let's get back to work.

276
00:15:23,256 --> 00:15:24,656
Looks like we have
a show to get on.

277
00:15:52,051 --> 00:15:54,363
Very nice... a little
awkward but very nice.

278
00:15:54,387 --> 00:15:57,380
You caught me.

279
00:15:58,891 --> 00:16:02,171
I was just going over
something in the police report.

280
00:16:02,195 --> 00:16:04,706
- What?
- Well, uh,

281
00:16:04,730 --> 00:16:08,777
- John Bosley Hackett's last meal.
- Excuse me?

282
00:16:08,801 --> 00:16:11,346
Well, of course he didn't know
it was gonna be his last meal.

283
00:16:11,370 --> 00:16:14,516
But according to
this breakfast order,

284
00:16:14,540 --> 00:16:18,954
he had orange juice, coffee,
pancakes, eggs, toast, syrup

285
00:16:18,978 --> 00:16:20,355
and grape jelly.

286
00:16:20,379 --> 00:16:24,159
And when, when the
police discovered his body,

287
00:16:24,183 --> 00:16:26,562
it seemed he'd eaten the eggs

288
00:16:26,586 --> 00:16:29,097
and toast and
pancakes and syrup,

289
00:16:29,121 --> 00:16:31,433
and the grape jelly,
but he hadn't touched

290
00:16:31,457 --> 00:16:32,935
the orange juice and the coffee.

291
00:16:32,959 --> 00:16:35,304
Isn't that going
at it backwards?

292
00:16:35,328 --> 00:16:36,805
Do you think he was killed

293
00:16:36,829 --> 00:16:38,540
because of his
bad eating habits?

294
00:16:47,573 --> 00:16:54,256
I'd like to, uh, get, uh,
something else off my chest.

295
00:16:54,280 --> 00:16:56,058
What?

296
00:16:56,082 --> 00:16:58,293
I don't think you
should try this case.

297
00:16:58,317 --> 00:17:00,863
Why?

298
00:17:00,887 --> 00:17:03,765
Because you're prejudiced,
you hated Sam's play.

299
00:17:03,789 --> 00:17:06,768
I'm not prejudiced and
I didn't hate the play.

300
00:17:06,792 --> 00:17:08,170
You said it stunk.

301
00:17:08,194 --> 00:17:11,206
I said it wasn't very
realistic, that's all.

302
00:17:11,230 --> 00:17:12,541
It's a musical.

303
00:17:12,565 --> 00:17:15,077
<i>The Phantom of the
Opera</i> isn't realistic.

304
00:17:15,101 --> 00:17:17,379
You're right, it stunk.

305
00:17:17,403 --> 00:17:20,202
See?

306
00:17:31,317 --> 00:17:32,327
Excuse me, Miss White?

307
00:17:32,351 --> 00:17:33,929
Hi. I'm Michelle Thomas.

308
00:17:33,953 --> 00:17:35,264
I represent Sam Spelvin.

309
00:17:35,288 --> 00:17:36,665
I was wondering if I could...

310
00:17:36,689 --> 00:17:39,167
Darling, you're
interrupting my process.

311
00:17:39,191 --> 00:17:42,638
Oh. I'm sorry.

312
00:17:42,662 --> 00:17:45,507
Your what?

313
00:17:45,531 --> 00:17:48,544
I'm concentrating
using sense memory.

314
00:17:48,568 --> 00:17:50,479
The artist's great tool.

315
00:17:51,671 --> 00:17:55,403
Oh, my God, that's
my cue! I'm on!

316
00:17:59,845 --> 00:18:03,926
♪ Well, I'm back from
the dean's office ♪

317
00:18:03,950 --> 00:18:06,328
♪ Had extra special tutoring ♪

318
00:18:06,352 --> 00:18:09,565
♪ No muss, no fuss,
I'm gonna get an A-plus ♪

319
00:18:09,589 --> 00:18:11,867
♪ Of that there's
no refutering ♪

320
00:18:11,891 --> 00:18:15,137
<i>♪ How did you do it? ♪</i>

321
00:18:15,161 --> 00:18:17,906
<i>♪ Show us the way ♪</i>

322
00:18:17,930 --> 00:18:21,009
♪ It's easy, you gals
are working too hard ♪

323
00:18:21,033 --> 00:18:23,879
♪♪

324
00:18:23,903 --> 00:18:26,214
♪ The way to make
a passing grade ♪

325
00:18:26,238 --> 00:18:28,050
♪ Depends a lot
on what you've got ♪

326
00:18:28,074 --> 00:18:31,253
♪ You gotta find
a teacher's aide ♪

327
00:18:31,277 --> 00:18:33,989
♪ And make him feel
he's hot, hot, hot ♪

328
00:18:34,013 --> 00:18:36,414
<i>♪ Hot, hot, hot ♪</i>

329
00:18:41,153 --> 00:18:43,498
♪ You really gotta
yearn to learn ♪

330
00:18:43,522 --> 00:18:46,101
♪ And teachers
show the way to go ♪

331
00:18:46,125 --> 00:18:49,237
♪ For every credit
that you earn ♪

332
00:18:49,261 --> 00:18:51,239
♪ Your grade is gonna
grow, grow, grow ♪

333
00:18:51,263 --> 00:18:56,712
♪ Making the grade,
making the grade ♪

334
00:18:56,736 --> 00:18:58,947
♪ You gotta make the grade ♪

335
00:18:58,971 --> 00:19:01,450
- High.
- Hello.

336
00:19:01,474 --> 00:19:04,886
No, I mean, high up.

337
00:19:04,910 --> 00:19:08,123
You always watch from here?

338
00:19:08,147 --> 00:19:10,559
Yeah, I like to
watch all of my shows

339
00:19:10,583 --> 00:19:12,848
from different
parts of the theater.

340
00:19:18,991 --> 00:19:21,703
Very, very nice, ladies.

341
00:19:21,727 --> 00:19:23,171
But I didn't understand a word.

342
00:19:23,195 --> 00:19:26,174
Can we take it from the top
and remember the words?

343
00:19:26,198 --> 00:19:28,377
They're part of
the plot, thank you.

344
00:19:28,401 --> 00:19:31,013
Okay, girls, let's do it again.

345
00:19:31,037 --> 00:19:33,582
- I love that piece.
- Oh, good.

346
00:19:33,606 --> 00:19:36,075
Yeah, that's one of my favorite
numbers in the whole show!

347
00:19:41,547 --> 00:19:44,459
Oh, oh, uh...

348
00:19:44,483 --> 00:19:48,797
I understand that you were up
here alone watching rehearsal

349
00:19:48,821 --> 00:19:50,165
when Mr. Hackett was killed.

350
00:19:50,189 --> 00:19:53,402
Yes, that's right.
Oh, except for Willie.

351
00:19:53,426 --> 00:19:54,770
Follow spot operator.

352
00:19:54,794 --> 00:19:55,937
Willie!

353
00:19:55,961 --> 00:19:57,539
Say hello to Mr. Matlock.

354
00:19:57,563 --> 00:19:59,998
Oh, hey there.

355
00:20:03,836 --> 00:20:06,806
♪♪

356
00:20:08,908 --> 00:20:11,420
Was there something else?

357
00:20:11,444 --> 00:20:14,990
Oh, no, no.

358
00:20:15,014 --> 00:20:17,959
No, that's, well, I
enjoyed talking with you.

359
00:20:17,983 --> 00:20:20,729
Oh, my pleasure,
glad you like our show.

360
00:20:20,753 --> 00:20:23,518
Yeah, like that number.

361
00:20:28,994 --> 00:20:31,707
♪ You really gotta
yearn to learn ♪

362
00:20:31,731 --> 00:20:34,076
♪ And teachers
show the way to go... ♪

363
00:20:38,871 --> 00:20:40,949
- and then this comes up!
- The show was about

364
00:20:40,973 --> 00:20:43,251
to close down, Guy, I
mean, I had to do something.

365
00:20:43,275 --> 00:20:44,753
You're supposed to check
with the producer, Sidney.

366
00:20:44,777 --> 00:20:46,822
It's elementary, you know that!

367
00:20:46,846 --> 00:20:48,957
But I did a good job, we had
a whole line around the block.

368
00:20:48,981 --> 00:20:50,859
I'll be the judge of whether
you did a good job or not.

369
00:20:50,883 --> 00:20:53,095
You had no right
running it, Sidney,

370
00:20:53,119 --> 00:20:54,796
without checking with me first.

371
00:20:54,820 --> 00:20:56,198
- But, Guy, you don't...
- Shh!

372
00:20:56,222 --> 00:20:59,090
- Guy, it was a great idea, Guy.
- Shut up!

373
00:21:02,928 --> 00:21:04,639
Can I help you, Mr. Matlock?

374
00:21:04,663 --> 00:21:06,942
Oh, well, I wanted to ask
you a couple questions.

375
00:21:06,966 --> 00:21:09,044
Oh, of course.

376
00:21:09,068 --> 00:21:11,333
Sid was just leaving.

377
00:21:15,641 --> 00:21:17,769
Ciao, Sidney.

378
00:21:19,578 --> 00:21:21,809
Come right in, Mr. Matlock.

379
00:21:28,687 --> 00:21:30,699
Well...

380
00:21:30,723 --> 00:21:36,471
Well, uh, you told the police
that you were in your office

381
00:21:36,495 --> 00:21:38,106
at the time of the murder.

382
00:21:38,130 --> 00:21:42,077
That's right, on the
phone with backers.

383
00:21:42,101 --> 00:21:46,214
Oh, so nobody saw you
or can vouch for you?

384
00:21:46,238 --> 00:21:50,852
I'm afraid not.

385
00:21:52,912 --> 00:21:56,558
Boy, you, you must have a
lot of money sunk in this show.

386
00:21:57,883 --> 00:21:59,194
No, no, no, no, no.

387
00:21:59,218 --> 00:22:02,164
Amateurs put their
own money in shows.

388
00:22:02,188 --> 00:22:04,533
- I have investors.
- Oh.

389
00:22:04,557 --> 00:22:07,769
Oh, they must've been
delighted when the publicity

390
00:22:07,793 --> 00:22:09,905
from the murder
started selling tickets.

391
00:22:09,929 --> 00:22:12,974
They're delirious.

392
00:22:12,998 --> 00:22:15,343
Mr. Matlock, I'm surprised.

393
00:22:15,367 --> 00:22:19,781
A great lawyer such as
yourself, beating around the bush.

394
00:22:19,805 --> 00:22:22,517
If you wanna know why I
had Sidney on the carpet,

395
00:22:22,541 --> 00:22:24,186
why don't you just ask?

396
00:22:24,210 --> 00:22:25,821
Why did you?

397
00:22:25,845 --> 00:22:30,258
I'm not in the theater
only for the money.

398
00:22:30,282 --> 00:22:34,329
My productions have
always been artistic.

399
00:22:34,353 --> 00:22:40,088
I don't like getting an audience
with cheap publicity tricks.

400
00:22:41,894 --> 00:22:46,631
If there's nothing else... No.

401
00:22:48,534 --> 00:22:51,231
Oh, well, ciao.

402
00:23:20,165 --> 00:23:23,158
♪♪

403
00:23:44,390 --> 00:23:46,234
Uh, you're leaving?

404
00:23:46,258 --> 00:23:48,336
Yeah, yeah, Guy said
he won't be needing me

405
00:23:48,360 --> 00:23:49,738
for the Atlanta
opening after all.

406
00:23:49,762 --> 00:23:51,439
- Oh?
- His way of saying you're fired.

407
00:23:51,463 --> 00:23:55,377
'Cause you broke
that story to the press?

408
00:23:55,401 --> 00:23:57,545
Yeah, and go figure,
he's the one who told me

409
00:23:57,569 --> 00:23:59,547
to get some critics to
Atlanta in the first place.

410
00:23:59,571 --> 00:24:02,284
Guy Palmer's a slippery old fox.

411
00:24:02,308 --> 00:24:05,854
If you ask me, something
weird is going on.

412
00:24:05,878 --> 00:24:10,178
Oh, uh, want-want a
cup-cup of hot coffee?

413
00:24:20,092 --> 00:24:23,187
I... Yeah, I had a great time.

414
00:24:24,897 --> 00:24:26,574
Good night.

415
00:24:29,935 --> 00:24:31,079
Brian?!

416
00:24:31,103 --> 00:24:32,247
Yes, sir.

417
00:24:32,271 --> 00:24:33,548
Read-through in the morning.

418
00:24:33,572 --> 00:24:34,716
Right.

419
00:24:34,740 --> 00:24:35,917
Party at my place.

420
00:24:35,941 --> 00:24:37,185
- Great show tonight, Keith.
- Thanks.

421
00:24:37,209 --> 00:24:39,120
Big improvement,
everybody. Thank you.

422
00:24:39,144 --> 00:24:40,635
Good night.

423
00:25:48,680 --> 00:25:51,309
Hey! Hey!

424
00:25:52,317 --> 00:25:54,062
What's going..?

425
00:25:54,086 --> 00:25:55,764
What are you...?

426
00:25:55,788 --> 00:25:58,433
Where's the other guy?
Where's the other guy?

427
00:25:58,457 --> 00:25:59,755
Ow!

428
00:26:04,129 --> 00:26:05,707
Hey!

429
00:26:07,800 --> 00:26:10,360
Oh.

430
00:26:22,381 --> 00:26:23,525
The show is wonderful.

431
00:26:23,549 --> 00:26:24,793
Let's go next week.

432
00:26:24,817 --> 00:26:27,028
Two in the orchestra, please.

433
00:26:27,052 --> 00:26:29,197
As I've already said,

434
00:26:29,221 --> 00:26:32,967
an artist's concentration
is essential to performance,

435
00:26:32,991 --> 00:26:36,257
and anything that disrupts
that concentration...

436
00:26:37,863 --> 00:26:41,176
I can't tell you how trying
this murder has been.

437
00:26:41,200 --> 00:26:44,045
Oh, I can imagine.

438
00:26:44,069 --> 00:26:47,130
But you've had trying
times before, haven't you?

439
00:26:48,674 --> 00:26:50,652
When?

440
00:26:50,676 --> 00:26:52,921
Well, for the past
several years,

441
00:26:52,945 --> 00:26:55,156
you've experienced, um,

442
00:26:55,180 --> 00:26:58,426
artistic rather than
commercial success.

443
00:26:58,450 --> 00:27:02,497
Are you saying I'm washed up?

444
00:27:02,521 --> 00:27:05,366
That the audience
won't pay to see me?

445
00:27:05,390 --> 00:27:08,069
That John Bosley Hackett,

446
00:27:08,093 --> 00:27:12,474
king of scum, hammered
the last nail in my coffin?

447
00:27:12,498 --> 00:27:14,943
That I would have done
anything to stop his review

448
00:27:14,967 --> 00:27:16,744
including murder him?

449
00:27:16,768 --> 00:27:19,180
Are you saying
that I'm a predator?

450
00:27:19,204 --> 00:27:21,015
A maniac on the loose?

451
00:27:21,039 --> 00:27:22,750
A black widow?

452
00:27:22,774 --> 00:27:24,486
Well?

453
00:27:24,510 --> 00:27:27,255
I don't think I said that.

454
00:27:27,279 --> 00:27:31,292
I didn't kill John
Bosley Hackett.

455
00:27:31,316 --> 00:27:35,463
I'm sorry, but... Oh, my.

456
00:27:35,487 --> 00:27:38,700
Where were you at
the time of the murder?

457
00:27:38,724 --> 00:27:41,302
I ask that exact
question in the play.

458
00:27:41,326 --> 00:27:43,905
Oh. Well, would you
mind answering mine?

459
00:27:43,929 --> 00:27:45,840
I was in my dressing room.

460
00:27:45,864 --> 00:27:47,976
Did anyone see you?

461
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,512
Do you realize how
offensive that question is?

462
00:27:50,536 --> 00:27:51,779
It implies I'm lying.

463
00:27:51,803 --> 00:27:53,014
Oh, I'm sorry.

464
00:27:53,038 --> 00:27:55,473
I didn't mean to upset you.

465
00:27:56,508 --> 00:27:58,153
I was alone.

466
00:27:58,177 --> 00:27:59,988
And you have my word on it.

467
00:28:00,012 --> 00:28:01,656
Sally Ayn?

468
00:28:01,680 --> 00:28:04,047
Mr. Palmer wants to
see you in his office.

469
00:28:06,118 --> 00:28:08,110
Good-bye.

470
00:28:12,524 --> 00:28:13,668
I'm half made up.

471
00:28:13,692 --> 00:28:15,069
What is it, Guy?

472
00:28:15,093 --> 00:28:17,619
Well, this will
only take a minute.

473
00:28:22,534 --> 00:28:25,613
Oh.

474
00:28:25,637 --> 00:28:29,517
A gathering of eagles.

475
00:28:29,541 --> 00:28:33,555
Why do I think
something is wrong?

476
00:28:35,147 --> 00:28:39,994
Honey... uh, Guy and I have
finally arrived at a really...

477
00:28:40,018 --> 00:28:43,898
I don't know... difficult,
mutual decision.

478
00:28:43,922 --> 00:28:49,037
I mean, uh... This
is... This is h-hard.

479
00:28:49,061 --> 00:28:51,072
Great.

480
00:28:51,096 --> 00:28:54,509
You shoot me in the front,
and you stab me in the back.

481
00:28:54,533 --> 00:28:55,810
You're gonna take
this act on the road?

482
00:28:55,834 --> 00:28:57,011
Honey.

483
00:28:57,035 --> 00:28:58,179
Say it, you coward.

484
00:28:58,203 --> 00:28:59,347
You're firing me.

485
00:28:59,371 --> 00:29:00,548
No.

486
00:29:00,572 --> 00:29:02,217
That speech about
sets was a lie.

487
00:29:02,241 --> 00:29:04,152
You're closing down
until you can replace me.

488
00:29:04,176 --> 00:29:06,254
All right.

489
00:29:06,278 --> 00:29:08,122
You <i>are</i> the reason
we're closing down.

490
00:29:08,146 --> 00:29:09,490
Uh, no.

491
00:29:09,514 --> 00:29:11,506
No, she isn't.

492
00:29:12,918 --> 00:29:17,498
A funny thing happened to me
on the way to the theater today.

493
00:29:17,522 --> 00:29:22,256
I... I learned, uh, what
happened to that set.

494
00:29:24,263 --> 00:29:26,608
I'm afraid I don't know
what you're talking about.

495
00:29:26,632 --> 00:29:27,942
Well, I can explain,

496
00:29:27,966 --> 00:29:30,959
but maybe, you
know, in-in private.

497
00:29:37,109 --> 00:29:39,020
Is this it? In here?

498
00:29:39,044 --> 00:29:40,221
Mm.

499
00:29:40,245 --> 00:29:42,056
Okay. Now...

500
00:29:43,548 --> 00:29:46,661
Do you feel at home
here, Mr. Matlock?

501
00:29:46,685 --> 00:29:47,895
Well, yeah.

502
00:29:49,921 --> 00:29:53,167
Now, about, uh...

503
00:29:53,191 --> 00:29:56,804
about Mr. Hackett and,
uh, your financial situation.

504
00:29:58,163 --> 00:29:59,507
Why is it the moment

505
00:29:59,531 --> 00:30:01,109
someone steps
foot in the theater

506
00:30:01,133 --> 00:30:02,610
they become melodramatic?

507
00:30:02,634 --> 00:30:04,712
Ah, I don't know.
There's such a...

508
00:30:06,138 --> 00:30:07,982
All right.

509
00:30:08,006 --> 00:30:09,984
I have financial problems,

510
00:30:10,008 --> 00:30:13,154
but I wouldn't kill
to have a hit show.

511
00:30:13,178 --> 00:30:17,274
Oh, a-a... a hit show is
the last thing you want.

512
00:30:18,483 --> 00:30:21,129
It... It-It-It
reminds me of a...

513
00:30:21,153 --> 00:30:23,164
of a... of a movie I saw once.

514
00:30:23,188 --> 00:30:25,233
I can't... I can't just
think of it... Wait...

515
00:30:25,257 --> 00:30:27,001
I don't follow.

516
00:30:27,025 --> 00:30:29,017
Well, here, look.

517
00:30:30,028 --> 00:30:31,839
Look. Uh...

518
00:30:31,863 --> 00:30:35,476
Marshall Turner
has 20% of the play,

519
00:30:35,500 --> 00:30:38,179
Dr. Richmond has ten percent.

520
00:30:38,203 --> 00:30:41,182
Uh, Mr. Doucette... 15.

521
00:30:41,206 --> 00:30:43,451
Mr. Carp... 30,
Mr. Young for 20.

522
00:30:43,475 --> 00:30:47,889
Mr. Niles for 20, and
Mr. Russo for 15, and...

523
00:30:47,913 --> 00:30:53,328
See... That adds up to 130%,

524
00:30:53,352 --> 00:30:55,430
and I'm only halfway
through the list.

525
00:31:00,492 --> 00:31:02,770
What does that got to do
with John Bosley Hackett?

526
00:31:02,794 --> 00:31:07,442
Well, you picked a play
that you figured was a turkey.

527
00:31:07,466 --> 00:31:09,977
Then you hired a
director and an actress

528
00:31:10,001 --> 00:31:11,713
at the bottom of their careers.

529
00:31:11,737 --> 00:31:14,916
And you budgeted the
show at a million dollars.

530
00:31:14,940 --> 00:31:18,486
And then you raised two
million dollars in backing.

531
00:31:18,510 --> 00:31:21,689
The only way you could
succeed was by failing,

532
00:31:21,713 --> 00:31:25,159
so you had Sid Falco
invite Mr. Hackett down

533
00:31:25,183 --> 00:31:26,627
to review your play.

534
00:31:26,651 --> 00:31:29,397
You needed his bad review,

535
00:31:29,421 --> 00:31:32,567
but his murder made it a hit.

536
00:31:34,760 --> 00:31:38,339
So you hired a couple of
thugs to tear up the scenery,

537
00:31:38,363 --> 00:31:41,175
and you fired Sally Ayn White.

538
00:31:41,199 --> 00:31:42,577
Oh, wait a minute.

539
00:31:42,601 --> 00:31:45,613
If I wanted it to be
a flop, I'd keep her.

540
00:31:45,637 --> 00:31:47,982
Hmm-mm. No, you just
wanted to delay the opening

541
00:31:48,006 --> 00:31:50,118
till the murder
publicity died down.

542
00:31:50,142 --> 00:31:53,121
Well, this plot would make a
very good play, Mr. Matlock.

543
00:31:53,145 --> 00:31:54,522
Yeah?

544
00:31:54,546 --> 00:31:58,292
But are you saying that I
killed John Bosley Hackett?

545
00:31:58,316 --> 00:32:00,328
No. No.

546
00:32:00,352 --> 00:32:02,897
You didn't have
motive or opportunity.

547
00:32:02,921 --> 00:32:06,901
What you did was,
uh, you destroyed

548
00:32:06,925 --> 00:32:09,170
a young writer's
play and his dream,

549
00:32:09,194 --> 00:32:12,473
and that's what I'll
see you in prison for.

550
00:32:12,497 --> 00:32:15,276
A young man's dream.

551
00:32:15,300 --> 00:32:17,945
Very nice, Mr. Matlock.

552
00:32:17,969 --> 00:32:21,315
What's the sentence
for destroying a dream?

553
00:32:21,339 --> 00:32:24,085
The same as fraud.

554
00:32:26,411 --> 00:32:28,756
Bravo.

555
00:32:28,780 --> 00:32:31,058
DeLuca, have you
been there all that time?

556
00:32:31,082 --> 00:32:32,393
Are you kidding?

557
00:32:32,417 --> 00:32:34,629
I wouldn't have missed
this for the world.

558
00:32:34,653 --> 00:32:36,864
What a scene.

559
00:32:36,888 --> 00:32:41,102
The staging was weak,
but content was riveting.

560
00:32:41,126 --> 00:32:42,870
What a drama.

561
00:32:42,894 --> 00:32:45,640
No, I take that back.

562
00:32:45,664 --> 00:32:47,475
A comedy.

563
00:32:47,499 --> 00:32:50,799
A really black comedy.

564
00:32:52,037 --> 00:32:57,418
Producer... sleazy
producer... Hires patsy

565
00:32:57,442 --> 00:33:00,421
director/choreographer
to play the fool.

566
00:33:00,445 --> 00:33:03,324
Had no choice but
to fire Sally Ayn.

567
00:33:03,348 --> 00:33:05,326
Really?

568
00:33:05,350 --> 00:33:06,527
Oh, get off it.

569
00:33:06,551 --> 00:33:07,862
You came off all right.

570
00:33:07,886 --> 00:33:11,232
If not for me, the chronically
unemployed Tommy DeLuca

571
00:33:11,256 --> 00:33:14,035
probably wouldn't have worked
again for another ten years.

572
00:33:14,059 --> 00:33:15,837
You're a bum.

573
00:33:15,861 --> 00:33:18,973
Mr. Palmer!

574
00:33:18,997 --> 00:33:20,241
It's Sally Ayn.

575
00:33:20,265 --> 00:33:22,410
I-I-I just found her on the
floor in her dressing room.

576
00:33:22,434 --> 00:33:24,528
She's OD'd.

577
00:33:42,053 --> 00:33:43,664
Call a paramedic.

578
00:33:43,688 --> 00:33:44,899
Sally Ayn?

579
00:33:44,923 --> 00:33:46,467
Are you all right?

580
00:33:46,491 --> 00:33:47,768
Honey.

581
00:33:47,792 --> 00:33:49,237
Honey, tell me you're all right.

582
00:33:49,261 --> 00:33:50,404
Get some coffee.

583
00:33:50,428 --> 00:33:51,639
Leave me alone.

584
00:33:51,663 --> 00:33:53,341
I want to die.

585
00:33:53,365 --> 00:33:55,309
You will in about 50 years.

586
00:33:55,333 --> 00:33:57,311
This is all my fault.

587
00:33:57,335 --> 00:33:59,113
If I hadn't been so relentless,

588
00:33:59,137 --> 00:34:01,148
If I hadn't-hadn't-hadn't...
pushed her so far.

589
00:34:01,172 --> 00:34:02,717
If you hadn't made me fire her.

590
00:34:02,741 --> 00:34:04,719
If you'd just bring the coffee.

591
00:34:04,743 --> 00:34:06,187
Tommy.

592
00:34:06,211 --> 00:34:08,322
Hold my hand, please.

593
00:34:08,346 --> 00:34:09,490
Oh.

594
00:34:09,514 --> 00:34:10,658
I feel so cold.

595
00:34:11,850 --> 00:34:13,361
Shh. Shh. Try to stay awake.

596
00:34:13,385 --> 00:34:14,529
Oh, I'm so tired.

597
00:34:14,553 --> 00:34:15,696
Just let me sleep.

598
00:34:15,720 --> 00:34:17,465
No! No, no.

599
00:34:17,489 --> 00:34:19,267
Will you get the coffee?!

600
00:34:19,291 --> 00:34:20,301
Oh.

601
00:34:23,628 --> 00:34:25,106
Oh.

602
00:34:30,035 --> 00:34:31,712
You fools.

603
00:34:31,736 --> 00:34:35,616
You call yourself a
producer, and you a director.

604
00:34:35,640 --> 00:34:37,485
Then you ought to recognize

605
00:34:37,509 --> 00:34:40,488
a great actress
when you see one.

606
00:34:40,512 --> 00:34:42,356
Then you're all right?

607
00:34:42,380 --> 00:34:47,161
For an aging
ingénue, I'm perfect.

608
00:34:47,185 --> 00:34:48,863
You were acting?

609
00:34:48,887 --> 00:34:51,165
This was a performance
good enough

610
00:34:51,189 --> 00:34:53,701
to convince an audience
two inches away.

611
00:34:53,725 --> 00:34:55,770
Stupid.

612
00:34:55,794 --> 00:34:57,204
You fired me.

613
00:34:57,228 --> 00:35:00,130
You have any idea what
you just put me through?

614
00:35:01,766 --> 00:35:04,211
You scared the hell
out of me, you phony.

615
00:35:04,235 --> 00:35:06,380
Me? Oh, no, darling.

616
00:35:06,404 --> 00:35:08,849
I'm the real thing.

617
00:35:08,873 --> 00:35:12,119
I've worked with Bob
Fosse and Michael Bennett.

618
00:35:12,143 --> 00:35:15,756
I've been toasted by
Andrew Lloyd Webber.

619
00:35:15,780 --> 00:35:20,761
And here's the picture of me
with Sam Spelvin, Guy Palmer

620
00:35:20,785 --> 00:35:24,699
and Tommy DeLuca the
morning of the murder.

621
00:35:24,723 --> 00:35:26,701
You're no Bob Fosse,

622
00:35:26,725 --> 00:35:29,971
but you seemed very fond of me.

623
00:35:29,995 --> 00:35:32,607
Whispering sweet nothings
and planning to fire me.

624
00:35:32,631 --> 00:35:35,910
I'm not the phony here, Tommy.

625
00:35:35,934 --> 00:35:38,379
You are a lying,
duplicitous creep!

626
00:35:38,403 --> 00:35:39,814
Well, I'll be dogged.

627
00:35:39,838 --> 00:35:42,984
Next time you play
an overdose scene,

628
00:35:43,008 --> 00:35:46,420
uh, try to get, you know,
sweaty and kind of clammy.

629
00:35:46,444 --> 00:35:47,455
It'll play better.

630
00:35:47,479 --> 00:35:48,723
Hmm.

631
00:35:48,747 --> 00:35:52,360
As for, uh, you and you, I'll...

632
00:35:52,384 --> 00:35:54,580
I'll see you gentlemen in court.

633
00:35:57,088 --> 00:36:00,534
<i>Uh, Mr. DeLuca,</i>

634
00:36:00,558 --> 00:36:02,770
John Bosley Hackett's review

635
00:36:02,794 --> 00:36:06,240
was not very
favorable to you, was it?

636
00:36:06,264 --> 00:36:10,177
Well, uh, he pretty
much savaged everyone

637
00:36:10,201 --> 00:36:12,413
- in the show, including me.
- Yeah.

638
00:36:12,437 --> 00:36:14,749
But this wasn't the first
time he'd reviewed a play

639
00:36:14,773 --> 00:36:16,951
you had directed, was it?

640
00:36:16,975 --> 00:36:20,087
Personally I don't read reviews.

641
00:36:20,111 --> 00:36:22,089
But, uh, I've directed
so many shows,

642
00:36:22,113 --> 00:36:24,058
- uh, and I guess he must have.
- Yeah.

643
00:36:24,082 --> 00:36:26,060
Well, this-this-this is a...

644
00:36:26,084 --> 00:36:29,563
This is a 15-year-old review.

645
00:36:29,587 --> 00:36:32,066
Uh, it's John-John
Bosley Hackett.

646
00:36:32,090 --> 00:36:33,134
See?

647
00:36:33,158 --> 00:36:36,137
And the play was
<i>Saint Joan of Rock.</i>

648
00:36:36,161 --> 00:36:38,139
You were the director.

649
00:36:38,163 --> 00:36:41,575
He hated the play and
he hated your direction.

650
00:36:41,599 --> 00:36:44,712
But he saved his most
devastating remarks

651
00:36:44,736 --> 00:36:46,747
for the actress.

652
00:36:46,771 --> 00:36:48,364
You remember her name?

653
00:36:49,407 --> 00:36:51,385
Jeanine Sewell.

654
00:36:51,409 --> 00:36:56,924
Jeanine Sewell DeLuca...
Your bride of six months.

655
00:36:56,948 --> 00:36:59,713
His review crushed
her, didn't it?

656
00:37:00,752 --> 00:37:02,897
She was pretty much destroyed.

657
00:37:02,921 --> 00:37:06,634
Would you tell the jury
what happened to her.

658
00:37:06,658 --> 00:37:10,538
After that review, she left me.

659
00:37:10,562 --> 00:37:12,373
She left her life.

660
00:37:12,397 --> 00:37:13,708
Fled to Europe and...

661
00:37:13,732 --> 00:37:17,111
The fact is that
she-she blamed you

662
00:37:17,135 --> 00:37:20,114
for her horrible
review, didn't she?

663
00:37:20,138 --> 00:37:21,682
Afraid so.

664
00:37:21,706 --> 00:37:24,685
And-and-and that's
why she left you.

665
00:37:24,709 --> 00:37:26,520
You could say that.

666
00:37:26,544 --> 00:37:27,822
Yeah.

667
00:37:27,846 --> 00:37:31,092
And you hated John
Bosley Hackett, didn't you?

668
00:37:31,116 --> 00:37:33,961
I mean, you-you blamed
him for the loss of your wife.

669
00:37:33,985 --> 00:37:37,131
And you waited for
15 years for revenge,

670
00:37:37,155 --> 00:37:39,133
and finally that
day came, didn't it?

671
00:37:39,157 --> 00:37:40,801
The perfect opportunity.

672
00:37:40,825 --> 00:37:42,269
Objection. Speculation.

673
00:37:42,293 --> 00:37:43,604
Sustained.

674
00:37:43,628 --> 00:37:45,139
The jury is directed

675
00:37:45,163 --> 00:37:47,508
to ignore the defense
council's last remarks.

676
00:37:47,532 --> 00:37:50,377
Mr. Matlock, please
confine yourself

677
00:37:50,401 --> 00:37:51,979
- to questions.
- Excuse me, Your Honor.

678
00:37:52,003 --> 00:37:54,014
Um, Mr. DeLuca, you were present

679
00:37:54,038 --> 00:37:56,851
when Mr. Hackett
told Sam Spelvin

680
00:37:56,875 --> 00:37:58,853
that he was going
to review his play

681
00:37:58,877 --> 00:38:01,021
even though it was
only a-a preview,

682
00:38:01,045 --> 00:38:02,556
and that he hated
it, weren't you?

683
00:38:02,580 --> 00:38:03,591
Yes, I was.

684
00:38:03,615 --> 00:38:05,926
And you were present
the next morning

685
00:38:05,950 --> 00:38:08,596
at the theater when you
learned that Sam was gonna go

686
00:38:08,620 --> 00:38:10,598
talk to Mr. Hackett,
weren't you?

687
00:38:10,622 --> 00:38:12,133
- Yes.
- Yeah.

688
00:38:12,157 --> 00:38:14,602
Would you tell the court what...
What you did right after that.

689
00:38:14,626 --> 00:38:17,104
I resumed rehearsal.

690
00:38:17,128 --> 00:38:19,907
Yeah, and you watched
from the balcony.

691
00:38:19,931 --> 00:38:21,675
Yeah, I was up there

692
00:38:21,699 --> 00:38:23,911
with, uh, Willy the-the
follow spot operator.

693
00:38:23,935 --> 00:38:25,946
But you-you didn't stay

694
00:38:25,970 --> 00:38:27,948
in the balcony the
whole scene, did you?

695
00:38:27,972 --> 00:38:29,984
Of course I did.

696
00:38:30,008 --> 00:38:33,154
Are you sure in the
darkness you didn't slip out?

697
00:38:33,178 --> 00:38:35,689
I just told you I was up there.

698
00:38:35,713 --> 00:38:37,725
- Ask Willy.
- I did.

699
00:38:37,749 --> 00:38:40,427
I had a-a long talk
with Willy the other day.

700
00:38:40,451 --> 00:38:43,998
And he tells me, that
a follow spot operator

701
00:38:44,022 --> 00:38:47,001
can't take his eyes off
the stage for a second.

702
00:38:47,025 --> 00:38:50,437
So he can't say whether
you stayed or left.

703
00:38:50,461 --> 00:38:51,639
Oh.

704
00:38:51,663 --> 00:38:53,507
So he didn't see
me for a few minutes.

705
00:38:53,531 --> 00:38:56,310
- Mm-hmm.
- Well, there's-there's no way

706
00:38:56,334 --> 00:38:59,346
that a person could have
gone over there, killed someone,

707
00:38:59,370 --> 00:39:02,149
and-and then come
back in that time.

708
00:39:02,173 --> 00:39:03,751
I disagree.

709
00:39:03,775 --> 00:39:06,420
A person could easily
have left that balcony,

710
00:39:06,444 --> 00:39:09,256
gone to the hotel,
killed Mr. Hackett,

711
00:39:09,280 --> 00:39:11,826
and gotten back
in plenty of time.

712
00:39:11,850 --> 00:39:14,261
Even me, bum knee
and all, I timed it.

713
00:39:14,285 --> 00:39:16,096
And a spry ex-dancer

714
00:39:16,120 --> 00:39:18,232
like Tommy DeLuca,
should have no problems.

715
00:39:18,256 --> 00:39:19,300
Don't you agree with that?

716
00:39:19,324 --> 00:39:21,068
No. I don't.

717
00:39:21,092 --> 00:39:23,070
Okay.

718
00:39:23,094 --> 00:39:25,739
Okay, look at this.

719
00:39:25,763 --> 00:39:27,741
Look at this.

720
00:39:27,765 --> 00:39:33,147
This... this is, uh,
Mr. Hackett's...

721
00:39:33,171 --> 00:39:36,183
breakfast menu for the
morning he was killed.

722
00:39:36,207 --> 00:39:39,520
See, he had... He
had orange juice,

723
00:39:39,544 --> 00:39:44,024
coffee, toast, eggs,
pancakes, syrup, grape jelly.

724
00:39:44,048 --> 00:39:46,427
The man had a fine appetite.

725
00:39:46,451 --> 00:39:49,296
But according to
the police report,

726
00:39:49,320 --> 00:39:52,299
when they found him, the only
thing that was left untouched

727
00:39:52,323 --> 00:39:55,135
was the orange
juice and the coffee.

728
00:39:55,159 --> 00:39:56,470
That seemed strange.

729
00:39:56,494 --> 00:39:58,305
I mean, why would a person...

730
00:39:58,329 --> 00:40:00,708
Why would a person
eat his-his-his toast,

731
00:40:00,732 --> 00:40:03,210
and his eggs, and his pancakes,

732
00:40:03,234 --> 00:40:04,979
and his syrup
and his grape jelly,

733
00:40:05,003 --> 00:40:07,014
and not touch the orange
juice and the coffee?

734
00:40:07,038 --> 00:40:09,234
Doesn't that seem odd?

735
00:40:10,275 --> 00:40:12,253
Not especially.

736
00:40:12,277 --> 00:40:13,821
Hmm.

737
00:40:15,847 --> 00:40:21,262
Uh, Mr. DeLuca, you wear,
um, a hairpiece, don't you?

738
00:40:21,286 --> 00:40:23,264
I beg your pardon.

739
00:40:23,288 --> 00:40:25,199
Uh, you know, a toupee, a wig.

740
00:40:25,223 --> 00:40:28,853
The-the conventional
kind held on with four clips.

741
00:40:30,361 --> 00:40:32,573
Well, you-you do, don't you?

742
00:40:32,597 --> 00:40:34,341
- Your Honor...
- I'll-I'll tie this together

743
00:40:34,365 --> 00:40:35,576
in a moment, Your Honor.

744
00:40:35,600 --> 00:40:36,777
Proceed.

745
00:40:36,801 --> 00:40:39,613
The-the man who
killed Mr. Hackett,

746
00:40:39,637 --> 00:40:42,516
slipped into his room,
and attacked him

747
00:40:42,540 --> 00:40:44,518
with a letter opener.

748
00:40:44,542 --> 00:40:48,022
Mr. Hackett tried
to defend himself.

749
00:40:48,046 --> 00:40:52,393
And he grabbed at his murderer
and pulled something off him,

750
00:40:52,417 --> 00:40:55,529
that fell into his breakfast.

751
00:40:55,553 --> 00:40:57,564
It was your wig, wasn't it?

752
00:40:57,588 --> 00:41:00,581
This is funny stuff.

753
00:41:01,859 --> 00:41:04,838
Who writes your material?

754
00:41:04,862 --> 00:41:06,974
I mean, uh, I think there's
a joke in there somewhere.

755
00:41:06,998 --> 00:41:08,466
I'm not joking!

756
00:41:09,667 --> 00:41:12,112
You were afraid
particles of your hairpiece

757
00:41:12,136 --> 00:41:14,949
would be found in the
eggs, the toast, the pancakes,

758
00:41:14,973 --> 00:41:16,350
the syrup, the grape jelly.

759
00:41:16,374 --> 00:41:18,385
So you flushed
it down the toilet,

760
00:41:18,409 --> 00:41:19,553
- didn't you?
- No!

761
00:41:19,577 --> 00:41:21,855
Then you rushed
back to your room.

762
00:41:21,879 --> 00:41:23,223
I mean, it would look very odd

763
00:41:23,247 --> 00:41:24,658
for you to come
out of that balcony

764
00:41:24,682 --> 00:41:27,294
with the eggs, and syrup
and grape jelly in your wig.

765
00:41:27,318 --> 00:41:30,698
So you put on another
one, and still got back

766
00:41:30,722 --> 00:41:32,733
before the end of the
dance number, didn't you?

767
00:41:32,757 --> 00:41:34,101
Objection!

768
00:41:34,125 --> 00:41:36,971
Mr. Matlock, this kind of
speculation must come to an end.

769
00:41:36,995 --> 00:41:38,973
I have pictures.

770
00:41:38,997 --> 00:41:40,641
Pictures.

771
00:41:40,665 --> 00:41:42,643
See?

772
00:41:42,667 --> 00:41:45,646
Two for you.

773
00:41:45,670 --> 00:41:49,149
<i>Two for you.</i>

774
00:41:49,173 --> 00:41:52,653
Two for you, sir.

775
00:41:52,677 --> 00:41:55,656
And two for us.

776
00:41:55,680 --> 00:42:01,462
Now, this first photo is of you,

777
00:42:01,486 --> 00:42:03,797
and Sally Ayn White
and Guy Palmer...

778
00:42:03,821 --> 00:42:05,799
Of course they don't spell it.

779
00:42:05,823 --> 00:42:07,801
Taken just before the murder.

780
00:42:07,825 --> 00:42:10,804
You see, your hairpiece
is very fashionable.

781
00:42:10,828 --> 00:42:12,840
See, how it's kind
of long over the ears.

782
00:42:12,864 --> 00:42:13,874
See there?

783
00:42:13,898 --> 00:42:15,209
- Yes.
- Okay.

784
00:42:15,233 --> 00:42:17,378
Now, in this-this
newspaper photo,

785
00:42:17,402 --> 00:42:19,680
it was taken as part of
the interview that you gave

786
00:42:19,704 --> 00:42:22,683
20 minutes after the murder.

787
00:42:22,707 --> 00:42:25,686
Your hair is shorter.

788
00:42:25,710 --> 00:42:26,920
See that sideburn?

789
00:42:26,944 --> 00:42:28,422
See that?

790
00:42:28,446 --> 00:42:29,790
It's shorter.

791
00:42:29,814 --> 00:42:31,825
How'd-how'd your
hair get shorter

792
00:42:31,849 --> 00:42:34,842
if you were just
sittin' in the balcony?

793
00:42:36,020 --> 00:42:39,548
You put on another
toupee, didn't you?

794
00:42:40,925 --> 00:42:44,204
You're accusing me of
murder based on a news photo?

795
00:42:44,228 --> 00:42:48,042
Uh, Mr. DeLuca, you-you have...

796
00:42:48,066 --> 00:42:50,477
three hairpieces.

797
00:42:50,501 --> 00:42:53,514
One looks like
you need a haircut.

798
00:42:53,538 --> 00:42:57,017
One looks like you
just got a haircut.

799
00:42:57,041 --> 00:42:59,420
And one looks like
it-it's growing out.

800
00:42:59,444 --> 00:43:01,455
And, I don't know,
it got me to thinking.

801
00:43:01,479 --> 00:43:03,457
All these hairpieces... I mean,

802
00:43:03,481 --> 00:43:05,292
how-how do you clean 'em?

803
00:43:05,316 --> 00:43:08,295
I mean, do you shampoo
them or is it soap and water?

804
00:43:08,319 --> 00:43:10,731
How do you do that?

805
00:43:10,755 --> 00:43:12,900
Soap and water.

806
00:43:12,924 --> 00:43:15,803
Oh, soap and-and-and water.

807
00:43:15,827 --> 00:43:19,640
Uh, is it, um, synthetic hair?

808
00:43:19,664 --> 00:43:20,908
Yes.

809
00:43:20,932 --> 00:43:23,644
What if all that sticky
stuff... You know, the eggs

810
00:43:23,668 --> 00:43:27,147
and toast and-and syrup
and-and grape jelly...

811
00:43:27,171 --> 00:43:30,851
Wouldn't come out
with soap and water?

812
00:43:30,875 --> 00:43:35,889
What if that synthetic hair
got so sticky and matted,

813
00:43:35,913 --> 00:43:37,724
that-that it was useless.

814
00:43:37,748 --> 00:43:39,259
What-what would you have done?

815
00:43:39,283 --> 00:43:41,662
I don't know. It never happened.

816
00:43:41,686 --> 00:43:45,165
I think it did, and I think
you took it to a wig maker

817
00:43:45,189 --> 00:43:49,169
to have the ruined patches
cut out and rewoven.

818
00:43:49,193 --> 00:43:51,505
You did it the very
next day, didn't you?

819
00:43:51,529 --> 00:43:52,539
No!

820
00:43:52,563 --> 00:43:53,974
And you can
check that with my...

821
00:43:53,998 --> 00:43:56,243
Top Copy... your wig
maker in New York.

822
00:43:56,267 --> 00:43:58,078
No, you wouldn't have done that.

823
00:43:58,102 --> 00:44:00,547
And there aren't that
many wig makers here,

824
00:44:00,571 --> 00:44:03,550
so it wasn't very
hard to find...

825
00:44:03,574 --> 00:44:05,752
Mr. Bruce.

826
00:44:05,776 --> 00:44:10,157
He is the person you
took your wig to for repairs.

827
00:44:10,181 --> 00:44:13,760
And he is prepared to testify

828
00:44:13,784 --> 00:44:17,598
that in the ruined
patches of that hairpiece,

829
00:44:17,622 --> 00:44:24,738
he found toast,
eggs, pancakes, syrup

830
00:44:24,762 --> 00:44:26,740
and grape jelly.

831
00:44:26,764 --> 00:44:30,701
Exactly Mr. Hackett's
last breakfast order.

832
00:44:32,737 --> 00:44:37,151
And that's pretty well the
way it happened, isn't it?

833
00:44:37,175 --> 00:44:40,988
I know that you've just
directed a courtroom play

834
00:44:41,012 --> 00:44:42,990
and you probably know

835
00:44:43,014 --> 00:44:45,159
that you don't have to
answer any more questions,

836
00:44:45,183 --> 00:44:47,828
even that last one,
without representation.

837
00:44:47,852 --> 00:44:50,864
And it's... it's a good idea.

838
00:44:50,888 --> 00:44:54,882
And, um...

839
00:45:02,066 --> 00:45:03,644
I gotta tell you something.

840
00:45:03,668 --> 00:45:06,647
I-I'm no expert,

841
00:45:06,671 --> 00:45:10,164
but I didn't agree with
John Bosley Hackett.

842
00:45:11,943 --> 00:45:14,310
I didn't think your
play was that bad.

843
00:45:17,348 --> 00:45:19,340
Defense rests.

844
00:45:24,956 --> 00:45:26,767
Your Honor, in light
of the new evidence,

845
00:45:26,791 --> 00:45:28,936
the state moves that all charges

846
00:45:28,960 --> 00:45:31,939
against the defendant,
Sam Spelvin, be dropped.

847
00:45:31,963 --> 00:45:33,507
So noted.

848
00:45:33,531 --> 00:45:35,709
In the matter of the <i>State of
Georgia v. Sam G. Spelvin,</i>

849
00:45:35,733 --> 00:45:37,644
this case is dismissed.

850
00:45:39,503 --> 00:45:42,316
Ben, I don't know
how to thank you.

851
00:45:42,340 --> 00:45:45,652
Take out all those jokes
about money-grubbing lawyers.

852
00:45:45,676 --> 00:45:47,487
But it's some of
my funniest stuff.

853
00:45:47,511 --> 00:45:48,689
I didn't laugh.

854
00:45:48,713 --> 00:45:49,890
- Okay.
- Okay?

855
00:45:49,914 --> 00:45:51,892
Okay, okay.

856
00:45:51,916 --> 00:45:53,727
Um...

857
00:45:53,751 --> 00:45:55,729
uh, how 'bout we go
somewhere for dinner

858
00:45:55,753 --> 00:45:57,898
and maybe a-a show?

859
00:45:57,922 --> 00:46:00,400
How about just dinner?

860
00:46:01,926 --> 00:46:04,404
Uh, that tune you're whistling.

861
00:46:04,428 --> 00:46:08,242
Isn't that "Love and
Justice Is Blind"?

862
00:46:10,768 --> 00:46:12,079
It is.

863
00:46:12,103 --> 00:46:15,082
Catchy, isn't it?

864
00:46:15,106 --> 00:46:18,085
You know that big
number that tall guy sings?

865
00:46:18,109 --> 00:46:21,443
How about give that to Sally?

